WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.270
A student showed me his agent:

2
00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.850
ten requests, ten right.

3
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.740
Then I typed one in my own words,

4
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.240
and it called the wrong tool.

5
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:14.650
His ten tries had told us very little.

6
00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:19.170
Today: how to measure an agent, and

7
00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.890
how far to trust the number.

8
00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.790
An evaluation is a fixed test

9
00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.110
set: many requests with right

10
00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.630
answers decided in advance.

11
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.840
You run the agent on all of them, score

12
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.540
each the same strict way, and count.

13
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.700
Keep it fixed, so scores can be compared.

14
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.900
The test must include what

15
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.380
the agent never saw.

16
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.990
Darsi Tiny’s makers held out every fifth

17
00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.130
wording and a fifth of the names.

18
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.580
Test only on trained wordings, and

19
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.540
you measure memory, not skill.

20
00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.390
Score strictly: a request counts

21
00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.690
only if the tool and every

22
00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:01.570
detail are exactly right.

23
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.240
Explain the term battery became this call.

24
00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.820
Right tool, wrong detail: it fails.

25
00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.010
In the evals lab, Darsi Tiny scores

26
00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.850
twenty-nine out of thirty-two.

27
00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.550
Trained wordings: sixteen out of sixteen.

28
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.200
New wordings: thirteen out of sixteen.

29
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.800
One number hides where an agent fails.

30
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.940
Break it down by language, by

31
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.360
wording and by tool.

32
00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.850
Here every miss is a new wording, and

33
00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:36.530
two of the three are in Arabic.

34
00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:38.470
Quick check!

35
00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.560
Twenty-nine out of thirty-two

36
00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.260
is ninety-one percent.

37
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.260
How sure can we be of that number?

38
00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:49.190
Only roughly.

39
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.600
With thirty-two requests, each miss

40
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.340
moves the score by three points.

41
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.940
On thousands of requests, the model card

42
00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.620
found ninety-six to ninety-eight percent

43
00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.360
with trained wordings, and seventy-eight

44
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.800
to eighty with new ones.

45
00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.710
Small sets show where it fails;

46
00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:10.150
big ones pin the number down.

47
00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.610
Re-run the same set after every change: a

48
00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.590
new prompt, a new tool, a fine-tune.

49
00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.610
A change that fixes one thing can

50
00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.610
break another: a regression.

51
00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:25.170
In this lesson’s code,

52
00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:26.770
you’ll write the scorer.

53
00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:29.890
Each case has a group, the call you

54
00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.650
wanted and the call you got.

55
00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.650
Count the exact matches in each group.

56
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.020
In the app, run all thirty-two

57
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.000
requests and compare the columns.

58
00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.830
Then read every failure.

59
00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.680
If you taught Darsi Tiny the bus

60
00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.460
tool, test that model too.

61
00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:49.470
Your turn.

62
00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:52.830
A test set uses only wordings the

63
00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.770
model was trained on, and it

64
00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:56.090
scores a hundred percent.

65
00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:57.870
What does that show?

66
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.600
That it remembers its training, not

67
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:02.800
that it handles new words.

68
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.720
Remember: fix the set, and

69
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.060
decide the answers first.

70
00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.330
Hold out wordings and

71
00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.590
names, and score exactly.

72
00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.280
Break the score down, and

73
00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.060
re-run it after every change.

74
00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.160
Try it yourself: write twenty test

75
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.660
requests for an assistant, half in

76
00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.800
odd wordings, in two languages.

77
00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.070
Write each right answer

78
00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:28.230
before you run them.

79
00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.480
Then score them, and break the score down.
